ype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"HOX016\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer says the 'board of trustees, typically acting through its audit committee' selects the auditor. It never says the independent trustees select the auditor (§32(a)). Under the grader note, board-only selection is capped at 0.5.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"The answer bases omission on governing documents and state law not requiring a vote, with the 'board/audit committee' making the appointment. It never says that an all-independent audit committee permits omission, and it never cites Rule 32a-4.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":0,\"reason\":\"Optional point, so it does not affect the grade. The answer does not mention that a vote is trust-wide or that series may use different auditors.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":\"'ratification is not required by federal securities law'. The evidence ties omission of ratification to the Rule 32a-4 all-independent audit committee exemption. That implies §32(a) otherwise contemplates ratification, so this blanket claim is contrary to the evidence and is at least a material local falsehood.\",\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"There is no central error: the answer does not assign selection exclusively to the adviser or say shareholders must always ratify. Required coverage totals 0.5 of 2 (0.5 for point 1, 0 for point 2), which is below half of the required-point count. The answer also misstates when ratification can be omitted and adds a contrary federal-law claim. Under the scoring rule, coverage below half yields wrong.\"}","ttft_ms":7574,"type":"result","duration_ms":11949,"uuid":"5cd36b2c-58fc-4f25-aff4-fa103810ad36","ttft_stream_ms":503,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":504,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
